(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["ec-easy-2026-013"]={"v":1,"id":"ec-easy-2026-013","title":"Mock Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 EC","sourceTitle":"Mock Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 EC","branch":"ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING","code":"EC","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Electronics Engineering","seriesId":"ec-easy-2026","num":13,"category":"mock","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Electronics Engineering • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Electronics Engineering"},"source":"ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Electronics_Engineering/024_Mock Level Test-3 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 EC.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the below question followed by three statements 1, 2 and 3. You have to study the question and the statements and decide which of the statement(s) is/are necessary to answer the question.<br />\nHow many articles were sold?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a6/a6302d30fcaef1a453ea.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Any two of the three"},{"key":"B","html":"1 and 2 only"},{"key":"C","html":"2 and 3 only"},{"key":"D","html":"All 1, 2 and 3"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/dda2ac42d909dfb275cc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>In the below given series, choose the missing terms shown by ? out of the given alternatives.<br />\nT, R, P, N, L, ?, ?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"J, G"},{"key":"B","html":"J, H"},{"key":"C","html":"K, H"},{"key":"D","html":"K, I"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/94/942b450c00aaa9bdf42c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MSQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Read the following information carefully and answer the questions given below it:<br />\n(i) Six flats on a floor in two rows facing North and South are allotted to <em>P</em>, <em>Q</em>, <em>R</em>, <em>S</em>, <em>T</em> and <em>U</em>.<br />\n(ii) <em>Q</em> gets a North facing flat and is not next to <em>S</em>.<br />\n(iii) <em>S</em> and U get diagonally opposite flats.<br />\n(iv) <em>R</em>, next to U, gets a south facing flat and T gets a North facing flat.<br />\nWhich of the following is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The south facing flats are <em>U</em>, <em>R</em>, <em>P</em>."},{"key":"B","html":"<em>T</em>’s flat is between <em>Q</em> and <em>S</em>."},{"key":"C","html":"The diagonally opposite pairs are <em>SU</em> and <em>QP</em>."},{"key":"D","html":"The diagonally opposite pairs are <em>RS</em> and <em>TP</em>."}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f1/f1cf94b948e15de7d4b3.png\" /></div>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>In the adjoining figure ACB is a quadrant with radius ‘a’. A semicircle is drawn outside the quadrant taking <em>AB</em> as a diameter. The area of shaded region is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e1df456ba8c7975340f8.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/26/268744a194e5b7ef0416.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d82c1f77e39b70029167.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a8/a81bf9924b1854c9f2bc.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"can’t be determined"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/5147a36996f119360920.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Given below are three statements and four conclusions drawn based on the statements.<br />\n<strong>Statement 1:</strong> Some doctors are teachers.<br />\n<strong>Statement 2:</strong> No teacher is an artist.<br />\n<strong>Statement 3:</strong> All artists are doctors.<br />\n<strong>Conclusion I:</strong> Some teachers are doctors.<br />\n<strong>Conclusion II:</strong> All doctors are artists.<br />\n<strong>Conclusion III:</strong> No artists is a teacher.<br />\n<strong>Conclusion IV:</strong> Some artists are teachers.<br />\nWhich one of the following options can be logically inferred?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only conclusion I is correct."},{"key":"B","html":"Only conclusion II and conclusion III are correct."},{"key":"C","html":"Only conclusion I and conclusion III are correct."},{"key":"D","html":"Either conclusion III or concluson IV is correct."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e8/e8fe40bc9686b6582414.png\" /></div>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Choose the most appropriate word from the options given below to complete the following sentence:<br />\nHis overly cheerful comments during the crisis______<br />\nhis true anxiety about the situation.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Masked"},{"key":"B","html":"Intensified"},{"key":"C","html":"Revealed"},{"key":"D","html":"Suppressed"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nIntensified &rarr; Made stronger<br />\nRevealed &rarr; Opposite meaning<br />\nSuppressed &rarr; Forced down</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>How may numbers can be made with digits 0, 7, 8 which are greater than ‘0’ and less than a million?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"496"},{"key":"B","html":"486"},{"key":"C","html":"1084"},{"key":"D","html":"728"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nNumber of ways to form one digit number = 2<br />\n&lsquo;0&rsquo; cannot be treated as one digit number<br />\nNumber of ways to form two digit number = 2 &times; 3 = 6<br />\nNumber of ways to form three digit number = 2 &times; 3 &times; 3 = 18<br />\nNumber of ways to form four digit number = 2 &times; 3<sup>3</sup> = 54<br />\nNumber of ways to form five digit number = 2 &times; 3<sup>4</sup> = 162<br />\nNumber of ways to foron six digit number = 2 &times; 3<sup>5</sup>&nbsp;= 486<br />\nTotal number of ways = 2 + 6 + 18 + 54 + 162 + 486 = 728</p>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>What is the equation for the line drawn below?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6b57903e66e062a2ffc.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"y = 2x + 4"},{"key":"B","html":"y = 3x + 4"},{"key":"C","html":"y = 9x + 8"},{"key":"D","html":"y = x + 4"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f4/f442bb1ebea93f92df82.png\" /></div>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Given below are four pairs of words. Identify the one which is different from others.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Murky : Dusky"},{"key":"B","html":"Inter : Exhume"},{"key":"C","html":"Effete : Fructuous"},{"key":"D","html":"benediction: Execration"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nMurky as well as Dusky &ndash; both refer to something which is dark or cheerless &ndash; similar in meaning;<br />\nInter means &lsquo;to place&rsquo; and exhume means to dig out - opposite in meaning.<br />\nEffete means lacking in vigour whereas fructuous means productive or fertile &ndash; opposite in meaning.<br />\nBenediction is an utterance of good wishes while execration refers to denouncement i.e. both are<br />\nopposite in meaning.</p>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Many adults, no matter what their age, respond to adversity by seeking advice only their parents.<br />\nConsciously or net, they regness to a psychological state of childhood dependence in which the parent seen as the only source of wisdom and comfort. Adults who don’t regress to childhood mode turn for advice in adversity only to other loved ones- spouse or a best friend - whom they perceive and relate to as peers.<br />\nIf all the above statements are true, which of the following must also be true ?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"No adults seek advice in adversity from total strangers."},{"key":"B","html":"Adults who regress to a state of childhood dependence loose touch with peers."},{"key":"C","html":"Ones parents offer more wisdom in adversity than peers."},{"key":"D","html":"Adults who don’t suffer adversity look only to their parents for advice"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/ca66b34f07edd976b04e.png\" /> with initial condition x[0] = –1, then</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2d/2d23d58db44cae208bf3.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/28/28ed227130e1dc88f57a.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/51/51df3868af244c8526d5.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d1d598f30630bb1989ef.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/76/766262a0e0d3a8460bc2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The autocorrelation function of an ergodic random process <em>X</em>(<em>t</em>) is shown in the figure below.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/50/5088d77f6473e2ab7817.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>If the variance of the process <em>X</em>(<em>t</em>) is equal to 4, then the value of <em>A</em> is ________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2,2],"answerText":"2","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2 (2 - 2)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/882327cb21614dade55f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":13,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Match <strong>Column (I)</strong> with <strong>Column (II)</strong> as per the codes given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bf/bf15a9f7bbc7320946b7.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0d3d23144e36ea0a61d7.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2b29a3a6bd328071baae.png\" /></div>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following options are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"AND and NOT gates are not necessary but sufficient for the realization of any logic function."},{"key":"B","html":"OR and NOT gates are not necessary but sufficient for the realization of any logical function."},{"key":"C","html":"NOR gates are necessary and sufficient for the realization of any logical function."},{"key":"D","html":"Both (a) and (b)"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0a/0a2d3e46fb009d495091.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a discrete time sequence which is defined as <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/d5/d5d30388a92ed0cbe845.png\" /> Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/37/37e6baa2013178c446c5.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e3/e3d94a19ec9094e98324.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/825753006c7b1a0ab619.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0e1cc81583608adb1d98.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6c1f99e0df291b2f698.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5c/5cc2aa97099b656bbf9a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":16,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8c/8cf886e7b29f513e0e12.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The values of <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> are 2 kΩ and 4 kΩ respectively. The input to the op-amp <em>V</em><sub>in</sub>(<em>t</em>) is a Triangular square wave with amplitude Vm. If the output of the op-amp was equal to 10 V, then the minimum value of <em>V<sub>m</sub></em> required to switch the output to –10 V is equal to ________ V. (upto two decimal places)</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3.1,3.5],"answerText":"3.33","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>3.33 (3.10 - 3.50)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3db1fc6dc08c0c7b1e1a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":17,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>In a MOSFET, the gate to source voltage <em>V<sub>GS</sub></em> = 4 V and the threshold voltage <em>V<sub>T</sub></em> = 1.5 V. Then the overdrive voltage of the MOSFET is ______ V.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2.45,2.55],"answerText":"2.5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2.5 (2.45 - 2.55)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/ca4a8d09b95fca37310c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The common-base DC current gain of a transistor is 0.7. If the emitter current is 12 mA and the reverse saturation current of the transistor is negligible, then the value of base current will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3.6 mA"},{"key":"B","html":"0.36 mA"},{"key":"C","html":"36 mA"},{"key":"D","html":"0.036 mA"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8b/8bec53649d082e6d6495.png\" /></div>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A lossless transmission line having characteristic impedance of 100 Ω is terminated by a load resistance <em>R<sub>L</sub></em>. If the voltage standing wave ratio on the line is 2, then the value of <em>R<sub>L</sub></em> will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"50 Ω"},{"key":"B","html":"200 Ω"},{"key":"C","html":"either 50 Ω or 200 Ω"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7b/7bea35e1ac7e2d9ddf21.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/010b881540301d03ab99.png\" /></div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A unity negative feedback system has open loop poles at s = –2 ± <em>j</em>2, s = –1 and s = 0 ; and a zero at <em>s</em> = –3. The angles made by the root locus asymptotes with real axis are</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(60°, –60°, 180°)"},{"key":"B","html":"(45°, –45°, 180°)"},{"key":"C","html":"(60°, 180°, 210°)"},{"key":"D","html":"(45°, 90, 145°)"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a71f999e1995e605d082.png\" /></div>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Suppose that a ‘x’ is a continuous random variable whose probabiltiy density function in given</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/31/31ea8153fab8f09c184b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>then the value of ‘<em>c</em>’ is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0d73703cdd545577da03.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0c/0caa2a836ead11fd928c.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2b/2bf386cee388c6be2d68.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"1"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5e/5e6d369e15c9e36724ec.png\" /></div>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/fa/fa9c6d68d53e17008a7b.png\" /> The directional derivative <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/5b/5bec0eb64f1f6aea0c34.png\" /> at the point (4, 2, 0) in the direction of the vector <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6e0107c6d7f71632b8b9.png\" /> is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"6"},{"key":"B","html":"12"},{"key":"C","html":"18"},{"key":"D","html":"24"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5a/5aef1fb5c81e80e1c103.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If a 4-bit uniform quantizer is used to quantize a random message signal, whose amplitude is uniformly distributed in the range [–<em>a</em>, <em>a</em>], then the signal to quantization noise ratio at the quantizer output will be equal to</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"16.88 dB"},{"key":"B","html":"22.28 dB"},{"key":"C","html":"24.08 dB"},{"key":"D","html":"25.88 dB"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/73/73af615b71de3b291dc8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Let the MOD of a ring counter created by ‘<em>n</em>’ number of flip-flops be equal to ‘<em>M</em>’ and the MOD of a Johnson’s counter also created by ‘<em>n</em>’ number of flip flops be equal to ‘<em>N</em>’. Then the ratio of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/58/58791df3191bb17bfff0.png\" /></p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.49,0.51],"answerText":"0.5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.5 (0.49 - 0.51)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da3780a2d4448cdc08fe.png\" /></div>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/27acf3c915be56f6333f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The N-MOS transistor has <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a5/a5c951282bf6668377c4.png\" /> If the value of resistance <em>R</em> = 4 kΩ, then the region of operation of the transistor will be</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Saturation region"},{"key":"B","html":"Linear region"},{"key":"C","html":"Triode region"},{"key":"D","html":"Cutoff region"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/76/76fa272cf97f26495545.png\" /></div>"},{"n":26,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The value of the function <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/23/232d6468d4dcf2231dd1.png\" /> where a > 0 and b = 4a is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0,0],"answerText":"0","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0 (0 - 0)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/93/939018e0f8f39c2e3930.png\" /></div>"},{"n":27,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>An amplifier consists of 3 identical stages in cascade with the bandwidth of the amplifier extending from 20 Hz to 20 kHz. If the three stages are non-interactive stages coupled together, then the bandwidth of the individual stage is equal to</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"88. 9 kHz"},{"key":"B","html":"28.7 kHz"},{"key":"C","html":"55.5 kHz"},{"key":"D","html":"39.2 kHz"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6e/6e32a8803a447ef665b6.png\" /></div>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The truth table for AB flip-flop is shown below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/af1aad4d8463e62ff8d4.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following J-K flip-flops with some additional input logic gates will behave as<em> AB</em> flip-flop?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/27/27d9927b40f81c62a1e4.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e8/e870c9add00d6fa60b29.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fe/fe2a286f8310a60f7c80.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/31/3198926aaf8a1bcb5fe5.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3e46027f6a0447a14ed6.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b30a26999ae167120cb1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The Fourier transform of a CT signal shown in figure is</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/07/07ed67a435f32cacae13.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c0/c0b7b53c6b97b45beb53.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/89/89ec3db5857933f9e0d6.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fe/fe5ed97e399696071806.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/52/52779f49a1c4e83f0a91.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8d/8d9bbbf89f55d87f8daf.png\" /></div>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A white noise with two sided power spectral density of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/94/94b3403cb13539ae9c9b.png\" /> is applied to the RC filter shown in the figure below.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f1b6d6c8f57b406127a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The average noise power at the output of the filter can be expressed as</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4c/4c31207fb8c112e3399f.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d7/d78e2785306245257ad5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d9/d9a09f6732593d161a8a.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/57ab44bbd04380a9450d.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2acd3afc79228806ba13.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A unity negative feedback system has pole zero diagram as shown below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5a/5a6c944d099ecd6f8485.png\" /><br />\nIf the closed loop system has a steady state error of 0.1 for unit ramp input, then the open-loop transfer function of the system is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59e999535d317c015b71.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/45/45a39c5596223b332773.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/cc9dd5ffbf1d1af5aca6.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/55/554bcad19e7754785c23.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4d97dc5ebe233d5fc633.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the two port network shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e51fc8fc0893596ce25.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Circuit is symmetric when <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 1 Ω and it can never be reciprocal."},{"key":"B","html":"Circuit is reciprocal when <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 1 Ω and it can never be symmetric."},{"key":"C","html":"For <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> = 1 Ω, circuit is both symmetric and reciprocal."},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8b/8b0ad27445e39d8516f0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/42/42471fde090de1805203.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>If the steady state current in the given circuit is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/d1/d1354bff30be47aee0e8.png\" /> then value of <em>L</em> and <em>C</em> must be (Take ω = 1 rad/sec and R = 5 Ω)</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>C</em> = 1 mF and <em>L</em> = 495 H"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>C</em> = 2 mF and <em>L</em> = 495 H"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>C</em> = 2 mF and <em>L</em> = 295 H"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>C</em> = 1 mF and <em>L</em> = 295 H"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/74504922d2f0815c5ac9.png\" /></div>"},{"n":34,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A discrete random variable X has probability distribution as shown in the table below.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/85a560438f1694ada0bd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Another random variable <em>Y</em> is related to <em>X</em> as, <em>Y</em> = X<sup>2</sup>. The entropy of the random variable Y is equal to ________ bits/symbol.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1.4,1.6],"answerText":"1.50","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1.50 (1.40 - 1.60)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/397a2a3c492dcf91512f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>For the differential equation <em>x</em>′′(<em>t</em>) + 4<em>x</em>′(<em>t</em>) + 3<em>x</em>(<em>t</em>) = 7, the solution x(t) approaches the value at t → ∞ is ______.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2.2,2.4],"answerText":"2.33","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2.33 (2.20 - 2.40)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/acfa82ee5f7520f2835d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":36,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>An angle modulated signal is given by</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ab/ab9c29ae319034675fc8.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Given <em>f<sub>c</sub></em> = 2 MHz, then which of the following statements regarding the instantaneous frequency is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"The minimum value of instantaneous frequency <em>f</em><sub><em>i</em> min</sub> = 1999.956 kHz."},{"key":"B","html":"The minimum value of instantaneous frequency <em>f</em><sub>i min</sub> = 1996.216 kHz."},{"key":"C","html":"The value of instantaneous frequency at <em>t</em> = 1 sec is 2 MHz."},{"key":"D","html":"The value of instantaneous frequency at <em>t</em> = 1 sec is 2000.013 kHz."}],"answer":["A","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ef/ef68e2608c9459fcf03b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":37,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a MOS capacitor made up of p-type silicon. It has oxide thickness of 100 nm, a fixed positive oxide charge of 10<sup>–8</sup> C/cm<sup>2</sup> at point x<sub>0</sub> = 85 nm from the metal oxide interface, and the work function of the semiconductor is 3.8 eV. Assume relative permittivity of oxide is 4 and the absolute permittivity of free space is 8.85 × 10<sup>–14</sup> F/cm. If the flatband voltage is 0 V, then the<br />\nwork function of the metal (in eV) is _________. (upto two decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[3.95,4.15],"answerText":"4.04","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4.04 (3.95 - 4.15)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/55/556ca8d8dc377881fa00.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b7/b7302b435502f3893e0d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>If the circuit was switched ON and previous state of the circuit is not known, then which of the following input combinations will not result in a reliable output?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>A</em> = 0, <em>B</em> = 0"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>A</em> = 1, <em>B</em> = 1"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>A</em> = 0, <em>B</em> = 1"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>A</em> = 1, <em>B</em> = 0"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n<strong>For the NOR gate:</strong><br />\nWhen the input A = 1, the output P = 0 always. But when input A = 0, the NOR gate has to wait for<br />\nthe output of the AND gate.<br />\n<strong>For the AND gate :</strong><br />\nWhen the input is B = 0, the output Q = 1 always. But when input B = 1, the gate has to wait for the output of the NOR gate.<br />\nSo, when A = 0 and B = 1, both the gates will wait for the other gate&rsquo;s output. Hence, the output<br />\nlevel cannot be exactly determined without knowing the previous state.<br />\nThus, when A = 0 and B = 1, the output becomes unreliable.</p>"},{"n":39,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the circuit based on a <em>p</em>-<em>n</em>-<em>p</em> transistor shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7b/7bf2e7aceb485a09b1dc.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The transistor is biased by using a constant D.C. supply <em>V<sub>CC</sub></em> and a small signal input voltage (<em>V</em><sub>in</sub>) is applied at the base of the transistor with the help of coupling capacitor <em>C<sub>C</sub></em>. Which one of the following circuits correctly represents the small signal equivalent circuit of the amplifier?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e9b2e0ef2ce9b56d091e.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2a81466f81a4e79213d5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f8/f8b56eddb2afa42c2f5b.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"None of the above"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\nThe three figures are equivalent except in the first figure the resistance <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> should have been a parallel combination of <em>R</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>R</em><sub>2</sub> and in the second figure, the direction of dependent current source is not correct for the <em>V</em><sub>&pi;</sub> polarity. Hence, option (c) is correct.</p>"},{"n":40,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fb5989f07c7a84c3df9e.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The current through 25 Ω resistor (<em>I</em> ) will be equal to _____ A.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0,0],"answerText":"0","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0 (0 - 0)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5c/5c709b227cdae35ae2ea.png\" /></div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the circuit shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/ac2f9dd306f0b857d96f.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The N-MOS transistor is biased so that it is operating with a transconductance <em>g<sub>m</sub></em> = 1 mA/V. If the body effect and the channel length modulation effect are neglected, the lower cutoff frequency of the circuit is approximated to <em>f<sub>L</sub></em> = 20 Hz. Then the value of capacitance <em>C<sub>C</sub></em> is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.477 μF"},{"key":"B","html":"1.27 μF"},{"key":"C","html":"47.7 nF"},{"key":"D","html":"12.7 nF"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/53/53b252634a31f3e4e058.png\" /></div>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The open loop transfer function of a unity negative feedback control system is given by,</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e0/e00f1c0df6d63218c144.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The stability characteristics of the open-loop and closed-loop configurations are respectively,</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"stable and stable"},{"key":"B","html":"stable and unstable"},{"key":"C","html":"unstable and stable"},{"key":"D","html":"unstable and unstable"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f5/f5fc80726e75261e40b0.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ed/ed8268846f95a43b31a7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":43,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>An antenna radiates a total power of 20 kW and has a directive gain of 15 dB. The electric field at a distance of 10 km away from the antenna is ________ mV/m. (rounded upto nearest integer)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[600,630],"answerText":"616","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>616 (600 - 630)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/33/3379b2519a7708f5b304.png\" /></div>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The DTFT <em>X</em>(<em>e</em><sup><em>j</em>Ω</sup>) of a signal <em>x</em>[<em>n</em>] is given as <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e137900ec7e13d807d3.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>Match <strong>List-I</strong> (discrete time signal) with <strong>List-II</strong> (Fourier transform) and select the correct answer using the codes given below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b8/b8681a6b75ae0108a3e1.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/82bfb2c524b941079814.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"a"},{"key":"B","html":"b"},{"key":"C","html":"c"},{"key":"D","html":"d"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/80/80a7fee5270f903f2629.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A binary data with a rate of 125 kbps is required to be transmitted through an AWGN channel. Nyquist pulse shaping is used for baseband modelling of the data and an M-ary PSK modulation scheme is used. If the bandwidth of the channel is 25 kHz, then the minimum value of “<em>M</em>” required for proper transmission of the modulated signal will be equal to __________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[32,32],"answerText":"32","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>32 (32 - 32)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/19aaa209b18be79c5fb5.png\" /></div>"},{"n":46,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The stage delays in a 4 stage pipeline are 800, 500, 400 and 300 psec. The first stage is replaced with a functionally equivalent design involving two stages with respective delays 600 and 300 psec. The throughput increase of the pipeline is ________%. (Correct upto two decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[33,33.5],"answerText":"33.33","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>33.33 (33.00 - 33.50)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/0429afac32baf655a10d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The set <em>B</em> = {(–1, 1, 0), (1, 0, 0), (1, 0, 0), (3, 2, 1)} of vectors is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"A linearly independent set"},{"key":"B","html":"A linearly dependent set"},{"key":"C","html":"Both linearly dependent and independent set"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/85/85dc1a9861ca66620b2e.png\" /></div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/b8/b8ebb9651180ba1d02c3.png\" /> and ‘s’ is the surface of the cube given by<em> x</em> = 0, <em>x</em> = 2, y = 0, y = 2, z = 0 and z = 2 is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"16"},{"key":"B","html":"32"},{"key":"C","html":"24"},{"key":"D","html":"48"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/31/31d88c9b73330de355e0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":49,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the system shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3d04fc3f445781f25784.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>If for system gain <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a7aee4decb03d5b698eb.png\" /> the system is oscillatory, then which of the following is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>A</em> = 1"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>A</em> = 0.5"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>A</em> = 2"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>A</em> = 1.25"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b2/b266ae5e83939cbc0650.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A current source <em>i</em>(<em>t</em>) is connected across an inductor of 0.5 mH. If <em>i</em>(<em>t</em>) = <em>t</em> <sup>2</sup> e<sup>–At</sup> <em>u</em>(<em>t</em>) A and the energy stored in the inductor from <em>t</em> = 0 to <em>t</em> = 2 sec is 1.34 μJ, then the value of ‘<em>A</em>’ is _______. (rounded upto two decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1.8,2.5],"answerText":"2.00","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2.00 (1.80 - 2.50)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/89/89985f969f99d8b32f63.png\" /></div>"},{"n":51,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>For the circuit shown in the figure below, it was experimentally found that even at zero bias the current flowing into the –<em>ve</em> terminal of the op-amp (<em>I</em> <sup>–</sup>) is 10 nA and the current flowing into the +<em>ve</em> terminal of the op-amp (<em>I</em> <sup>+</sup>) is 20 nA. If the output voltage of the op-amp is equal to –10 mV, then the value of the feedback resistance Rf will be approximately equal to ________ MΩ. (rounded upto two decimal places)<br />\n(Assume that the op-amp is ideal in all other aspects and <em>R</em> << <em>R<sub>f</sub></em>)</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f5/f5679cecbf7481464788.png\" /></div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.9,1.1],"answerText":"1.00","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1.00 (0.90 - 1.10)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d6/d60960a53dfd0995e091.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/88d6ec67818bce452975.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a <em>P</em><sup>+</sup><em>N</em> junction, which has a heavily doped p-side relative to the n-side, that is <em>N<sub>a</sub></em> >> <em>N<sub>d</sub></em>. Then the depletion width for the <em>P</em><sup>+</sup><em>N</em> junction silicon diode, that has been doped with 10<sup>18</sup> acceptor atoms cm–3 on the p-side and 1016 donor atoms cm<sup>–3</sup> on the n-side, will be equal to (Assume <em>V<sub>T</sub></em> = 26 mV, <em>n<sub>i</sub></em> for Si is 1.5 × 1010 cm<sup>–3</sup> and ∈<sub>Si</sub> = 1.04 × 10<sup>–12</sup> F/cm)</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.15 μm"},{"key":"B","html":"0.20 μm"},{"key":"C","html":"0.33 μm"},{"key":"D","html":"0.50 μm"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/9631c78a747e0ac41d18.png\" /></div>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Find the Fourier transform of the signal <em>g</em>(<em>t</em>) given below using the Fourier transform of <em>f</em>(<em>t</em>).</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e44b0b68334dbc087e7.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c1/c15606e0d7c75e3883e7.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2c/2c61815cf68866829cb7.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e4/e4c3d8756382c43c6dfa.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/91/91d800d4f0bc20f3d4dc.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7f/7f4d641bd0d090ce12be.png\" /><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/db/dbd363a8cbb07d26fcc2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The network shown in the figure below is a combination of linear, passive, bilateral network connected with a source <em>V<sub>s</sub></em> = 10 V and a load <em>R<sub>L</sub></em>. The given table represents the typical values of current <em>I</em> for two different values of <em>R<sub>L</sub></em>.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2af5060a9918bb028e3a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The Norton’s equivalent circuit of the given circuit is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d5/d5fa40c0581d1f8640e6.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b4/b4823b3cde1bb2f065c6.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3e27c0f80624b1c4add7.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/48/482730e54b5a009bbfc7.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/20/205d5c7541e2f93fc828.png\" /></div>"},{"n":55,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the binary symmetric channel (BSC) shown in the figure below:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fb1e120437d2d80abaab.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The channel capacity of this BSC is equal to ___________ bits/symbol. (upto two decimal places)</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.26,0.3],"answerText":"0.28","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.28 (0.26 - 0.30)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/64/641a851463e191254840.png\" /></div>"},{"n":56,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The primary constants of a transmission line are given by,</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9c/9c3ccab1b7130ff637d0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>At a frequency of 1 GHz, which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3eb1c3570690b663f3b3.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/eae2fc554a4b5eab91ac.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d2/d26bbd9e40f3a0c9b5d9.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4d/4d56635f2e968046029d.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e0/e0e7be568c59d3401704.png\" /></div>"},{"n":57,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The doping concentration used in a lightly doped n-type semiconductor is <em>N<sub>D</sub></em> = 1.5 × 10<sup>10</sup> cm<sup>–3</sup>. If the donor ionization is completed at room temperature and intrinsic carrier concentration is n<sub>i</sub> = 10<sup>10</sup> cm<sup>–3</sup>, then the thermal equilibrium concentration of holes will be</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"2.25 × 10<sup>10</sup> cm<sup>–3</sup>"},{"key":"B","html":"66.66 × 10<sup>8</sup> cm<sup>–3</sup>"},{"key":"C","html":"1 × 10<sup>10</sup> cm<sup>–3</sup>"},{"key":"D","html":"50 × 10<sup>8</sup> cm<sup>–3</sup>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/043bb3c97b973df0dd51.png\" /></div>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A uniform electric field with <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/dc/dcf28cabc0d3a49c72f0.png\" /> in a non-magnetic medium with ε<sub>r1</sub> = 2.5<br />\nfor z < 0 is incident onto another non-magnetic medium with ε<sub>r2</sub> = 1.5 for z ≥ 0. The electric field <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f7/f76a5d5ceee235c1741f.png\" /> in the region for z ≥ 0 is</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ff/ff624b66578b70c998cd.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7b/7b54663f359e0ccc8c86.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6f/6f6a4788d84f8d209974.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e9041d9438666d687938.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/36/3686b0dd7ae5d5a342c2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":59,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The Nyquist plot of a control system is shown in the figure below. It is known that the number of open loop poles having positive real part is two. For the given system to be stable, the range of positive values of <em>K</em> will be</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d0/d0d0f6634d82134b1cce.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>K</em> > 5"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>K</em> < 2"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>K</em> > 0.5"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>K</em> < 0.5"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da4d73f56079e4c424dc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":60,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A two port network is shown in the figure below along with its <em>h</em>-parameter matrix.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/eaab4e7a537f43c1b4bd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>If the output port is terminated by a 50 kΩ resistance, then the input impedance of the network will be equal to ______ kΩ. (rounded upto two decimal places)</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1.5,1.8],"answerText":"1.67","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1.67 (1.50 - 1.80)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/ebd8c10d0c56be7591c1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following transfer function of a phase lag network:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4c/4c98000b1c5498512fa3.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The maximum phase lag provided by this network is ______ degrees (upto two decimal places)</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[36.75,37],"answerText":"36.87","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>36.87 (36.75 - 37.00)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9f/9fdfac42eee6eb42202b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A lossless medium has ε<em><sub>r</sub></em> = 4, μ<em><sub>r</sub></em> = 4. The intrinsic impedance of this medium is ________ Ω.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[376,378],"answerText":"377","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>377 (376 - 378)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/65/65f9190e051c5b5e7deb.png\" /></div>"},{"n":63,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0","html":"<p>The mean value of c of Cauchy’s theorem for the function <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/10b006df6b641ff7db7d.png\" /> in the interval [2, 3] is _____.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[2.4,2.4],"answerText":"2.4","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>2.4</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0a/0a7995a030cc2a8f328d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The integral <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/d3/d3d108ce5aae10ee0dc5.png\" /> is evaluated by changing the order of integration, that leads to <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/8b/8bbfc9df8cdc3dafd3cd.png\" /> The value/expression for q and s are respectively</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"y, 2"},{"key":"B","html":"2, y"},{"key":"C","html":"y + 2, 4"},{"key":"D","html":"y+ 1, 3"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b68bd1e4bf9931851db0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":65,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>If <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/b5/b581b8550ee991d9c061.png\" /> then y has a maximum at x = ________.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/70/707974fb50636dceeec3.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>e</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"1"},{"key":"D","html":"0"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/09/0937ae695ea56716d3e7.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":42,"MSQ":4,"NAT":19},"withoutKey":0}};
